<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
<overview>
<p>
Merging a recursive attribute set with the <code>//</code> update operator is
surprising. The <code>rec { ... }</code> block is evaluated in its own recursive
scope, so references inside it do <b>not</b> see attributes supplied by the
other operand of the merge.
</p>
<p>
Readers often assume that a later override changes what the recursive bindings
mean, but it does not. This can leave the merged attrset in a partially
initialised, misleading state (CWE-665).
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Avoid mixing <code>rec { ... }</code> with <code>//</code>. If later attributes
must depend on the merged result, compute the merge in a surrounding
<code>let</code> binding and derive dependent attributes from that explicit
value instead.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>Unsafe — <code>b</code> still uses the original <code>a</code> from the recursive block:</p>
<sample language="nix">
let
  merged = rec {
    a = 1;
    b = a + 1;
  } // {
    a = 41;
  };
in
  merged.b
</sample>
<p>
The result is <code>2</code>, not <code>42</code>, because <code>b</code> was
bound inside the left-hand <code>rec</code> scope before the merge.
</p>
<p>Safe — merge plain attrsets first, then derive dependent attributes explicitly:</p>
<sample language="nix">
let
  merged = {
    a = 1;
  } // {
    a = 41;
  };
in
  merged // {
    b = merged.a + 1;
  }
</sample>
</example>

<references>
<li>
Nix Reference Manual:
<a href="https://nix.dev/manual/nix/latest/language/operators">Operators</a>
</li>
<li>
CWE: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/665.html">CWE-665: Improper Initialization</a>
</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
